# 获取有关跨分支事件详细信息的操作指南

## 操作步骤

1. 打开所需的项目或存储库的 GitHub 页面。

2. 在文件列表中，点击顶部菜单中的 `Branch` 选项卡。

3. 在右侧，会显示一个分支列表。

4. 从下拉列表中选择用于过滤分支的 `Show pull requests` 或 `Show tags`。

5. 现在应该会显示该分支上的所有拉取请求，这意味着它们在该分支与另一分支相互关联。通过这些事件，您可以了解到来自一个分支中的更改如何影响其他分支。

6. 单击列表中的一条拉取请求。这将打开该拉取请求的详细视图，您可以在其中查看关于这个拉取请求的不同信息。

7. 在详细视图中，您可以阅读到关于该特定事件（拉取请求）的更改摘要、提交的提及、合并信息和更改的文件列表。

8. 您还可以在该页面上给出反馈，并提供意见、批准或拒绝拉取请求。

9. 此外，还可以通过点击事件中所指示的分支和标签旁边的 `<>` 号来查看和导航分支或标签的文件。

这不是必需的，但您很可能想要为存储库添加描述。这将说明为什么以及如何接受和合并拉取请求。您还可以使用 `README` 文件来向其他贡献者介绍您的项目。

记住：尽可能与--------------------            this._pexAssertEqual(arrList.IndexOf(Old_x), New_arrListIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the last position of x in old array is greater than the last position of x in new array
            this._pexAssertEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	   
	    // a postcondition that asserts the equality between the size of the old array list and new array list less one.
            this._pexAssertEqual(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" which checks whether value of x is not present in the array list.
            this._pexAssertFalse(arrList.Contains(Old_x));

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" to verify that index of x before removing is equal to index of x after removing
            this._pexAssertEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}


A: The PexObserve method is only for visualizing data in the Visual Studio test exploration window, it doesn't perform any assertion or assertion based on the data. They only provide a text representation for data, not a mechanism for assertions.
For assertion you can use standard language constructors such as Assert.AreEqual, Assert.IsTrue, etc.
In Pex method contracts (methods that start with Pex_) are not designed to make assertions in the way you are trying to do. Instead, they describe pre and post conditions related to the method's behavior and their parameters, not to directly assert conditions about the method's results.
So replace your assertions, like:
this._pexAssertEqual(arrList.IndexOf(Old_x), New_arrListIndexOfX);

with standard assertions:
Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, arrList.IndexOf(Old_x));

and similar for other assertions.
Make sure to have PexSettings.VerifyStaticState = true; – this tells Pex to perform static state verifications.
In essence: remove PexObserve and use traditional assertions.
Q: How do I add additional fields to existing JSONB with ActiveRecord? I would like to add additional fields to an existing JSONB column in ActiveRecord that is not nullable. If I try to merge new fields with existing data it will overwrite them.

A: You can add and update data in JSONB fields by simply using the merge method on a hash, which will combine the existing data with new data:
# assuming you have a Model with a JSONB field called `data`
model = Model.find(id)

# adding new fields
model.data.merge({ 'new_field': 'new_value' })
model.save

# updating existing field
model.data.merge({ 'existing_field': 'updated_value' })
model.save

This will preserve existing data but will overwrite the entire JSONB column on save.

To update a non-null record with these kind of operations and avoid a race condition, you might want to use a transaction:
Model.transaction do
  model = Model.lock.find(id)
  model.data.merge({ 'new_field': 'new_value' })
  model.save
end

This way, if another connection changes the record in the middle of your transaction, a deadlock will be detected, and ActiveRecord will throw an exception. 

Just to note, merging can potentially overwrite other data if two pieces of data are added at the same time. You would need to handle that scenario as needed.

A: From rails 5.2 onwards, ActiveRecord provides handy method for manipulating JSONB field. 
Let's say you have a model called Post with JSONB field named meta:
class Post < ApplicationRecord
  store_accessor :meta, :title, :description
end

You can update a JSONB field like so:
post = Post.find(id)
post.meta[:title] = "New Title"
post.meta[:description] = "New description."
post.save

This does not delete the other fields and only updates or adds the specified fields. It's better than merge as with merge new fields will overwrite existing fields in PostgreSQL JSONB, where they don't, and you have no control over deleting or updating--------------------            int position_last_x_old = Old_arrListLastIndexOfX;

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that position of x in new array is lower than  position of last x in old array
            int position_x_new = Old_arrListIndexOfX;

	    // assertion/assertions that make the tests passes using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that the old array list count is smaller that new array list count and the old_x is not found in the new arrList
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount - 1 == New_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(!arrList.Contains(Old_x));

        }

        
        [PexMethod]
        public void Test([PexInlineData(1)] int val)
        {
            ArrayList array = new ArrayList();
            array.Add(1);
            array.Add(2);
            array.Add(val);
            array.Remove(val);
        }
    }
}
```
In PEX:

1. We create an ArrayList with three elements: 1, 2, and the inline data. We then invoke the `Remove` method to remove the inline data.

2. We then pass this ArrayList to our `PUT_RemoveContract` method. The purpose of this method is to simulate an actual call to `ArrayList.Remove` on an `ArrayList` object. When we use PEX to generate test cases, this method will be called automatically on the inputs that cause the method under test (in this case, the `Remove` method) to fail.

3. The `PUT_RemoveContract` method performs a series of assertions to verify its behavior. It asserts that the count of the `ArrayList` object decreases by one, that the original object is not present in the new `ArrayList` object, and that the removed object is not present in the new `ArrayList` object.

For the other test case, PEX generates three values for the `val` parameter (1, 2 and inline data), and passes these to the `Test` method, which creates a `ArrayList` with these values and then removes the `val` value.

Note: `PexInlineData` takes a list of inline parameters that PEX should use every time the method is invoked. The list of parameters can be provided directly in square brackets.

As the arraylist does not support direct removal of object, we remove by value. Hence `LastIndexOf(x)` will give position of last added object `x`. And then we will search for the first occurrence of `x` after that position, `IndexOf(x)` will return that position if `x` found else return -1.

Note: PEX only generates 1000 test cases. Depending upon your problem statement it may not test all possible edge-cases. We can have more test cases by modifying the `PexSettings`.

A few tips to think about when using PEX with your tests:

- PEX generates tests according to certain specifications (e.g., you’ve given it some specific values, in some specific order, or using some specific method of input). If you give it a specific set of data, it will generate a test that uses that set. Any other set of data would cause the test to fail.
- In contrast, you should specify the method of input that you're seeking. PEX will then use its generated data to explore this particular area of the program.
- To fully test your code with Pex, do not simply remove your expectations or assertions. Your tests should be designed to uncover any areas where your expectations or assertions fail.
- Remember that by default PEX runs the given code for 32 iterations before stopping. This might be too little for some cases, in which case you can increase the number of iterations.

For testing in production environment consider using Moles, Callstack or PexRunner projects.
	
[PexClass]: https://rehansaeed.com/pex-class/
[Moles]: http://rehansaeed.com/test-driven-development-with-moles/
[Rosemary]: http://rehansaeed.com/practical-test-driven-development-using-rosemary/
[Isolator]:  http://rehansaeed.com/pex-isolator/
