It seems that the PexAssumeUnderTest does not work for ArrayList. The ArrayList object gets initialized but subsequent assertions always fail because of incorrect index values. 

I am unsure what is the right way to test methods that alter the state of objects in an array format using Pex.

A: It seems to be a problem with the verification logic rather than the ArrayList itself or the array list method itself.

The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to indicate that the method or class under test is an implementation with the assumption that every instance of the actual type is a subtype or the same type.

As per the documentation, the attribute uses reflection to get all types that inherit from the actual type, and it will only run a test on each of these types. Then, it uses PexChoose for the actual type and then refines the type based on the values returned by PexChooser. 

Although using PexAssumeUnderTest with a data structure like ArrayList should work, it seems in your case, it might not behave as expected in the contracts, because although the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute doesn't provide invalid parameters, it could lead to the incorrect states. 

It's important to note that ArrayList objects are a special type that Pex cannot handle correctly. If you are going to use such methods with Pex, you should avoid using PexAssumeUnderTest with ArrayList objects.

You may need to use the [PexIgnore] attribute to ignore certain situations if Pex does not handle the object correctly or adjust your approach to testing that object if you only want to test your methods, not their implementation details. 

You may also want to consider testing other collection types that Pex can handle such as List, Queue, Stack, etc.

Lastly, I cannot offer a definite solution without knowing the exact error or failing tests, so some trial+error may be necessary.

References:
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/pex/msr-tr-2010-8/ 

Hope this helps your issue.

A: My understanding is that ArrayList is the .NET implementation for a list (or array) in which an item can be added, removed or accessed by index. Testing mutable object like the ArrayList is hard because PEX relies on immutability properties of state for checking its properties. 

The PexAssumeUnderTest does not work for ArrayList

It does not work because ArrayList is not a simple testable type. ArrayList is a complex type with many properties - not just a collection. So, Pex has trouble understanding what state changes can be applied or what state invariants should hold in an ArrayList object, especially when those changes are method calls that modify the state.

I am unsure what is the right way to test methods that alter the state of objects in an array format using Pex.

Your approach can be to isolate your tests to the basic operations (Add, Remove, Get, IndexOf) and use different approaches to cover the edge cases - such as using lists of various sizes, trying to remove an object that doesn't exist in the list, trying to remove an object from different indices in the list, etc. Testing methods that alter the state of an object in an array format becomes a bit harder if it's the ArrayList object. You might be better of with an array or a simpler List-based collection type. If you specifically want to test ArrayList, you can use PexIgnore to tell Pex to skip the tests and not create or analyze tests for that type.

In a nut shell, the PexAssumeUnderTest won't help in this case because it's a wrong tool for the job.

Also, you may find useful this post - https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/jaredpar/2008/09/16/pex-gotchas/
Where the author discusses some common problems you might encounter with Pex when dealing with complex types like ArrayList or List.

A: I understand that ArrayList doesn't behave correctly with Pex, but there are some workarounds:
If Pex still complains about ArrayList, then consider testing the add, remove and get methods separately from the class. Test the class as a black box with input parameters only. You'll have to ensure that all methods involved are also tested separately, but the overall verification would probably be better than what might be achieved with PexAssumeUnderTest.
Another option is to use another collection type, such as List<T--------------------	    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            // an assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that the count of the old list is greater than the count of the new list
	    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount > New_arrListCount);

            // an assertion using "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that the method "remove" always remove the object, so if the object exist in the original array, then it should not exist after remove method is called
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, !New_arrListContainsX);

            // an assertion for ArrayList class using the IsTrue method that, after an object is removed from the list, the index of that object in the arraylist will always be less than the old index.
	    Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);

            // an assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(.*?\);" that the last index is always greater or equal to the original last index
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

