A: Here is a possible approach for your contract test:
The error you are getting is because of a type mismatch. The IndexOf method you are calling is supposed to work with an object and not an integer. You need to cast the integers to objects if you are planning to use them in IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods.

[PexMethod]
[PexCustomAttribute(typeof(ArrayListContractTest), "PUT_RemoveContract")]
public void TestRemove([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    Old_x = x;

    if (arrList.Contains((object)x))
    {
        Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf((object)x);
        Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf((object)x);
        Old_arrListContainsX = true;

        arrList.Remove((object)x);
    }

    New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf((object)x);
    New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf((object)x);
    New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains((object)x);
}

This version will work if x is of type integer and you have added PexObserve to help with verification.
Please note that the PexObserve here are not helpful as they would show the values of the parameter x at PUT_RemoveContract before and after the code execution. They do not help in verifying post-conditions of the method Remove. A more sophisticated PexVerify or PexObserve may be needed in combination with PexMethod under a different scenario.
Also, please note that for this test to work, the input array list (arrList) should not contain null items before the test is run. 
I noticed that you have a condition on checking if the array list contains x before removing, this might be unnecessary especially when you have PexAssumeUnderTest. Ensure you have a valid set of inputs or test this separately.
I hope these provide an insight into what you are trying to achieve.

A: The IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods take objects as arguments, not int. This is probably the source of the error you're experiencing. Also, you probably want to avoid using assumptions for this kind of testing. You should always provide actual inputs where possible.
You need to cast integer x to object prior to removing via arrList.Remove(x as object). The index of x in the old list should be lower or equal to the index in the new list. This implies the index should be preserved or increased after removing the element. 
Finally, be careful to test if the array list contains the item prior to removing it. It seems you already do it, but to make sure, you can update your condition: Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains((object)x); 
Hope this helps.

