## Remarks

Contracts like these will be run every time we perform a Pex operation on the ArrayList. Pex checks that the method's precondition and postcondition are maintained. By "precondition and postcondition are maintained," we're actually talking about the Assertions.

Let's break down the properties that the Assertions are checking:

- An assertion that checks that the last index of an element in the list before the operation is not less than the position of this element in the list after the operation.
- An assertion that checks that the last index of the element in the list before the operation is equal to the index of this element in the list after the operation.

- An assertion that checks that if the list contains the element before the operation, then it is guaranteed to no longer be in the list after the operation.
- An assertion that checks that the list count before the operation is one less than the list count after the operation.
- An assertion that checks that if the element is removed from the array list, then it's not longer in the list (contains is false).

When Pex founds any violations, it will report the error that it encountered in a detailed manner, pointing out exactly what the issue was. By seeing the specifics, developers can go straight to the source of the problem.

## Conclusion

Pex provides an impressive way to find and fix issues early on in our development process. It has proven to be a very valuable tool both for bug detection and for refactoring. If you have any other questions, do not hesitate to ask.

